# Triangles: Classification, Congruence, and Similarity

> The triangle is geometry's atom — rigid, simple, everywhere. Its
> angle sum, its inequality, and its famous theorem let you measure
> heights, prove equality, and scale shapes with confidence.

## 1. Classifying Triangles

@diagram tree id="triangle-families" layout="tree" aria="Triangles classified by sides and by angles"
  "Triangles" -> "By sides: scalene, isosceles, equilateral"
  "Triangles" -> "By angles: acute, right-angled, obtuse"
@/diagram

| Family | Meaning | Key fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scalene | all sides different | all angles different |
| Isosceles | two sides equal | base angles equal |
| Equilateral | all sides equal | all angles 60° |
| Acute | all angles < 90° | — |
| Right | one angle = 90° | hypotenuse is the longest side |
| Obtuse | one angle > 90° | side facing it is the longest |

## 2. The Angle Sum Is Always 180°

**Angle-sum property:** the three interior angles of any triangle add
to 180°. Proof sketch: through one vertex draw a line parallel to the
opposite side; the two alternate angles created match the remote
interior angles, and with the vertex angle they sit on a straight line.

[definition]
**Exterior angle theorem:** an exterior angle equals the sum of the
two remote interior angles — 40° and 65° give an exterior angle of
105°.
[/definition]

## 3. The Triangle Inequality and Pythagoras

Any two sides of a triangle must sum to **more** than the third — so
3, 4, 9 cannot form a triangle (3 + 4 = 7 < 9), and the third side
of a triangle with sides 5 and 8 lies strictly between 3 and 13.

**Pythagoras' theorem:** in a right triangle, the square on the
hypotenuse equals the sum of squares on the other two sides.

| Triple | Check |
| --- | --- |
| 3, 4, 5 | 9 + 16 = 25 |
| 5, 12, 13 | 25 + 144 = 169 |
| 8, 15, 17 | 64 + 225 = 289 |
| 7, 24, 25 | 49 + 576 = 625 |

Multiples are triples too: 6-8-10 and 9-12-15 are just 3-4-5 scaled.

[example]
A 13 m ladder leans on a wall with its foot 5 m out. The wall side is
√(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12 m — the 5-12-13 triple in the wild. The
**converse**: if a² + b² = c², the angle facing c is exactly 90° —
so an 8 × 15 rectangle has a 17 m diagonal.
[/example]

## 4. Congruence: Same Shape AND Size

Two triangles are **congruent** when one is an exact copy of the other.

| Criterion | You need | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SSS | all three pairs of sides | |
| SAS | two sides + the angle **between** them | angle must be included |
| ASA | two angles + the side between | |
| AAS | two angles + any corresponding side | |
| RHS | right angle, hypotenuse, one side | right triangles only |

[warning]
SSA is **not** a criterion — two different triangles can share two
sides and a non-included angle. AAA guarantees similarity only: angles
fix shape, not size. After proving congruence, use **CPCT** —
corresponding parts of congruent triangles — to claim any remaining
matching side or angle.
[/warning]

## 5. Similarity: Same Shape, Any Size

Triangles are **similar** when angles match (AA), all three pairs of
sides are in one ratio (SSS), or two sides are in one ratio with the
included angle equal (SAS). For a similarity ratio k:

- corresponding sides scale by k, perimeters by k, **areas by k²**.

[example]
A triangle with sides 3, 4, 5 meets a similar partner whose shortest
side is 6, so k = 2: the new sides are 6, 8, 10 and the perimeter
doubles to 24. If the small one has area 6, the big has 6 × 2² = 24 —
areas grow by the square of k.
[/example]

## 6. Parallel Lines and Proportion (Thales)

The **basic proportionality theorem** (Thales): a line parallel to one
side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio — if
AD/DB = 3/2, then AE/EC = 3/2. The converse holds, and the midpoint
theorem follows: the segment joining midpoints is half the third side,
parallel to it.

## 7. The Four Centres of a Triangle

| Centre | Lines that meet there | Location |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Centroid | the three **medians** | always inside; divides each median 2 : 1 from vertex |
| Incentre | the three **angle bisectors** | always inside; centre of the inscribed circle |
| Circumcentre | perpendicular bisectors of sides | inside (acute), midpoint of hypotenuse (right), outside (obtuse) |
| Orthocentre | the three **altitudes** | inside (acute), at the right-angle vertex (right), outside (obtuse) |

A **median** joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side; an
**altitude** is perpendicular to it. The circumcentre is equidistant
from the three vertices, the incentre from the three sides.

## 8. Area Formulas

@math label="eq:triangle-areas"
A = \tfrac{1}{2} b h
\qquad\qquad
A = \sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)},\ \ s = \tfrac{a+b+c}{2}
@/math

[example]
**Heron's formula** for sides 13, 14, 15: s = (13 + 14 + 15)/2 = 21,
so A = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6) = √7056 = 84 square units. The altitude on
the 14-side satisfies ½ × 14 × h = 84, so h = 12 — a cross-check.
[/example]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Angles 40°, 65° — third angle? | 75° (sum is 180°) |
| Ladder 13 m, foot 5 m out? | wall height 12 m |
| Congruence criteria? | SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, RHS |
| Sides scale by k — areas scale by? | k² |
| Sides 13, 14, 15 — area? | 84 (Heron, s = 21) |
